## Spotlight
Proteins are fundamental molecules that mediate diverse biological processes, and protein design can shed light on the molecular mechanisms underlying their biological functions. Huang and colleagues have developed a sequenceindependent statistical model for de novo protein design using neural networks (NNs) to learn the distribution of backbone structures with minimal side-chain information.
Life depends on the proteins that regulate diverse important physiological processes. Protein design can contribute to our basic biochemical understanding of life and enhance our ability to build the biological parts for synthetic biology. The goal of protein design is to generate a sequence of amino acids that can fold into a defined structure and execute desired functions. Over the past decades, significant progress has been made in both the methodology and practice of computational protein design (CPD). Scientists have successfully designed enzymes that can catalyze non-natural reactions [1], protein logic gates [2], and miniprotein inhibitors [3]. However, in common CPD practice, the protein backbones are generally assembled using natural fragments or are defined with strict geometric constraints, which has resulted in limited structural diversity. Thus, the development of novel methodologies to explore the potential backbone diversity of de novo designed proteins is a topic of intense interest. Liu and colleagues recently presented a statistical model named SCUBA (side chain-unknown backbone arrangement) that uses NNs applied to energy terms and stochastic dynamics (SD) to explore the protein backbone structure space [4]. This model has led to the development of new computational tools that can facilitate the design of proteins from a unique structural sketch (Figure 1).
To date, an all-atom energy function is usually applied to score the total energy of a designed sequence that has folded into a given backbone structure. However, if the design fails, it is unclear whether the structure itself is undesignable or the sequence space was not sufficiently sampled. To solve this problem, the authors proposed the SCUBA model to score a given structure backbone without side-chain information and optimize the backbone with significantly better designability. In their scheme, neighbor counting (NC) was adopted to estimate the probability density of the protein backbone structure space, and NNs were trained to represent the statistical potential. In this way, SCUBA was able to optimize an initial sketch structure without explicit atomic-level side-chain information to an appropriate designable structure.
