More specifically, in this work, the authors designed a two-step NC–NN process to parameterize the SCUBA model. First, NC was used to estimate the statistical energy from protein structural data. This statistical energy was then used as the labels in a second NN training step. In this way, the NN learned the statistical potential as a differentiable function of the backbone coordinates which can enable sampling and optimization of backbone structure. There are five distinct types of features, and each is the input of a specific NN: $(\mathfrak{j})$ the local dihedral angles of the residue, (ii) the dihedral angles of five consecutive residues, (iii) the translation and rotation between and the dihedrals of each residue pair separated by $>$ five residues with a Cα distance within $9.5\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ (iv) local hydrogen
Interestingly, before this work, the authors had devised the ABACUS (a backbonebased amino acid usage survey) model using a statistical energy-based method for fixed backbone protein design [5,6]. By splitting the energy of each residue into a single-residue term and the sum of the interactions between the different residues, the amino acid side-chain types were counted in a given backbone structure feature space. The total energy also included the main chain-dependent rotamer distribution and the interatomic distance distribution. ABACUS2 was thereafter further optimized in this work to increase the amino acid composition diversity of the newly designed proteins.
Utilizing SCUBA and ABACUS2 together, the authors propose a general de novo design workflow as follows. First, an initial sketch of the protein backbone skeleton is generated based on peptide geometries with a user-preferred approach (e.g., the sketch is automatically generated with a peptide geometry-aware computer program or manually drawn using molecular graphics software). Second, SCUBA is used to perform a SD simulation on the initial structure to obtain a designable backbone. Finally, the exact amino acid sequence can be selected using ABACUS2. The authors verified this workflow by experimentally testing several batches
