of proteins with different backbones [4]. Remarkably, all nine experimentally resolved structures of de novo designed proteins agreed with the designed models at atomic accuracy. It is worth noting that, even though the computational models were learned from known structures of natural proteins, four of the designed proteins contain novel folds that have not yet been observed in natural protein structures.
The design of novel backbone structures from scratch is highly challenging because the sampling space of the backbone and the sequence multiplication are too vast to converge and the underlying physical laws are highly complex. The authors have elegantly demonstrated that an NN can be effectively used as an energy function approximator to describe the potential energy surface of the protein structure space. As such, the SCUBA model could effectively facilitate the backbone-level jump which has been unattainable by conventional design methods.
Moreover, SCUBA-driven design has demonstrated that a vast unexplored protein structure space remains unexplored. Although genetic sequence data are growing exponentially, the discovery of novel protein structure topologies is not increasing at the same rate. With the development of the protein structure prediction, greater structural diversity has been predicted from metagenomic data [7]. Beyond evolution, SCUBA provides another innovative bottom-up strategy for exploring the designable protein structures that can further open up a larger space for the identification of their diverse and important biochemical functions.
